# nx-electron-vite Knowledge Base

> **Critical**: This file must be used EVERY TIME when working on nx-electron-vite project documentation, development, or support. It contains the complete architectural and technical understanding required for accurate work.

## Project Overview

**nx-electron-vite** is an Nx plugin that enables desktop application development by wrapping existing frontend applications in Electron shells within Nx monorepos. The plugin follows a distributed architecture pattern with explicit separation between "host" (Electron shell) and "guest" (frontend application) projects.

### Core Identity

- **Package Name**: `@erickrodrcodes/nx-electron-vite`
- **Purpose**: Nx Plugin to develop, package and build Electron projects inside Nx workspaces
- **Author**: Erick Rodriguez
- **License**: MIT
- **Repository**: https://github.com/ErickRodrCodes/nx-plugins

### Key Differentiators

- **True Framework Agnosticism**: Works with ANY framework supported by Nx (React, Angular, Vue, Svelte, SolidJS, etc.)
- **Distributed Architecture**: Host/Guest separation enforced by Nx project boundaries
- **Monorepo-First Design**: Built specifically for Nx monorepo environments
- **Vite Integration**: Uses Vite for both main and renderer processes with HMR
- **Native Module Isolation**: Architectural solution to native dependency conflicts

## Architectural Philosophy

### Distributed vs Monolithic Architecture

**Traditional Monolithic Electron Architecture Problems:**

- Script-driven development with accumulated npm scripts
- Implicit coupling between main/renderer/preload processes
- Configuration drift and project-specific complexity
- Reactive problem-solving leading to technical debt
- Framework lock-in despite claims of broad support
- Poor native module handling
- Inefficient build cycles

**nx-electron-vite Distributed Architecture Benefits:**

- **Principled Separation with Enforced Boundaries**: Nx project graph enforces architectural boundaries at tooling level
- **Multi-Target Deployment**: Guest applications can use different Nx executors for web, mobile, and desktop
- **Architectural Native Module Isolation**: Native modules contained within host project dependency tree
- **Monorepo-Scale Applications**: Multiple Electron apps sharing common libraries
- **True Framework Agnosticism**: Guest exists as independent Nx project using any framework
- **Standardized Development Patterns**: Generator-driven workflow ensures consistency

### Host/Guest Model

**Guest Project (Frontend Application):**

- Standard frontend application running as renderer process
- Completely framework-native and deployment-agnostic
- Can be developed, tested, and served as standalone web application
- Optional desktop integration through preload script APIs
- 100% framework-pure with zero Electron knowledge

**Host Project (Electron Shell):**

- New application created by `setup-project` generator
- Responsible for application window and main process logic
- Exposes controlled APIs to guest through preload script
- Contains native modules and desktop-specific functionality

**Communication Layer:**

- Well-defined contract through preload script
- Prevents unintended coupling
- Makes integration points explicit and testable

## Technical Architecture

### Plugin Structure

```
packages/nx-electron-vite/
├── src/
│   ├── generators/          # Code generation tools
│   │   ├── init/           # Plugin initialization
│   │   ├── setup-project/  # Electron project scaffolding
│   │   └── build-native/   # Native module integration
│   ├── executors/          # Build and deployment tools
│   │   ├── build-electron/ # Electron app building
│   │   └── build-icons/    # Icon generation
│   └── util/              # Shared utilities and versions
├── generators.json         # Generator definitions
├── executors.json         # Executor definitions
└── documentation/         # Comprehensive documentation
```

### Core Dependencies

```json
{
  "electron": "^30.0.2",
  "electron-builder": "^24.13.3",
  "@electron/rebuild": "^3.7.1",
  "vite-plugin-electron": "^0.28.6",
  "vite-plugin-electron-renderer": "^0.14.5",
  "png2icons": "^2.0.1",
